WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.134 --> 00:00:03.036 align:start size:84% position:16%
- You realize that
this world is becoming

00:00:03.036 --> 00:00:04.337
more and more tech focused.

00:00:04.337 --> 00:00:05.505
And cities have two options.

00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:07.374 align:start size:84% position:16%
They can either
embrace that reality.

00:00:07.374 --> 00:00:12.379 align:start size:97% position:3%
Or they can ignore it to their
peril of their residence.

00:00:12.379 --> 00:00:14.948
(upbeat music)

00:00:19.686 --> 00:00:20.687 line:0%
- All right, I'm Dave Rubin.

00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:23.223 line:0%
We are on location in Miami.

00:00:23.223 --> 00:00:26.860
With Miami mayor Francis Suarez.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:29.195
Mr. Mayor first off.

00:00:29.195 --> 00:00:31.431 align:start size:94% position:6%
The shirt matches the
shoes, matches the socks.

00:00:31.431 --> 00:00:34.434 align:start size:84% position:16%
You are living in
the synth wave future

00:00:34.434 --> 00:00:36.403
that was promised to us
in the eighties, huh?

00:00:36.403 --> 00:00:37.637
- That's right, that's right.

00:00:37.637 --> 00:00:38.471
This is Miami for you.

00:00:38.471 --> 00:00:40.273
It's Miami style.

00:00:40.273 --> 00:00:41.241
Absolutely.

00:00:41.241 --> 00:00:42.509
- Definitely Miami style.

00:00:42.509 --> 00:00:43.843
I mean, I guess the best
way to start this off.

00:00:43.843 --> 00:00:45.979
Is that this place
is just blowing up.

00:00:45.979 --> 00:00:48.515
You gotta be kind of feeling
good about life and the world

00:00:48.515 --> 00:00:49.749
and what you're doing.

00:00:49.749 --> 00:00:50.917
- Yeah.

00:00:50.917 --> 00:00:51.885 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Is that the
easiest question ever

00:00:51.885 --> 00:00:53.053
been asked to you before?

00:00:53.053 --> 00:00:54.487 align:start size:88% position:13%
- It's the most common,
that's for sure.

00:00:54.487 --> 00:00:56.022
Yeah, no.

00:00:56.022 --> 00:00:58.158
It really is, I think a
generational opportunity.

00:00:58.158 --> 00:00:59.292
A generational moment.

00:00:59.292 --> 00:01:00.627
Miami was always a great place.

00:01:00.627 --> 00:01:02.729
I mean, pre this
sort of tech boom.

00:01:02.729 --> 00:01:04.364
We were growing significantly.

00:01:04.364 --> 00:01:08.301 line:0%
We're not a well kept secret.

00:01:08.301 --> 00:01:11.271 line:0%
But I think what
is happening now is

00:01:11.271 --> 00:01:13.540
order of magnitudes
is different, right?

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:17.043
You're now creating a kind
of ecosystem and economy.

00:01:17.043 --> 00:01:19.579
That's really gonna propel
our city into the future.

00:01:19.579 --> 00:01:20.814
The world is changing.

00:01:20.814 --> 00:01:22.849
It's changing at a
pace of disruption.

00:01:22.849 --> 00:01:24.317
That we've never seen before.

00:01:24.317 --> 00:01:26.553 align:start size:97% position:3%
And we're talking about this
a little bit off camera.

00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:30.156
Cities just don't
get it, oftentimes.

00:01:30.156 --> 00:01:32.525 align:start size:94% position:6%
They don't understand
that things are changing.

00:01:32.525 --> 00:01:34.094 align:start size:84% position:16%
And they're changing
permanently.

00:01:34.094 --> 00:01:35.829
And they have to adapt quickly.

00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:37.030
- When did you get it?

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:39.099 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean, did you know
about this sort of shift

00:01:39.099 --> 00:01:42.602
before you were elected in
2017 or did this just happen?

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:45.805 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So I was actually elected
in 2009 to the council.

00:01:45.805 --> 00:01:46.639
As a councilman.

00:01:46.639 --> 00:01:48.108
And for about 10 years.

00:01:48.108 --> 00:01:50.376 align:start size:97% position:3%
We were trying to create
this technological ecosystem.

00:01:50.376 --> 00:01:54.280 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm a young elected
official for a major city.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:55.415
I'm 43.

00:01:55.415 --> 00:01:56.883
I was 32 at the time.

00:01:56.883 --> 00:01:59.519
And I realized that I
was a first generation

00:01:59.519 --> 00:02:01.020
with a smartphone.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:04.491
I was a first-generation
with laptops, cell phones.

00:02:04.491 --> 00:02:08.528
And you realize that our
world is more and more

00:02:08.528 --> 00:02:11.064
engulfed by technological tools.

00:02:11.064 --> 00:02:13.800 align:start size:97% position:3%
I now have a seven-year-old
and a three-year-old.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.369 align:start size:97% position:3%
And my three-year-old just the
other night was telling me,

00:02:16.369 --> 00:02:17.804
"Dad, let's take a selfie."

00:02:17.804 --> 00:02:21.241
With her, we call it
"Gutete" in Spanish.

00:02:21.241 --> 00:02:23.176
With her pacifier.

00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:24.344
She's taking a selfie with me.

00:02:24.344 --> 00:02:25.778
So three years old.

00:02:25.778 --> 00:02:26.980
So you realize-

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:29.549 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Cameras and pacifiers,
it's found it's way.

00:02:29.549 --> 00:02:30.617
- It's coming, it's coming.

00:02:30.617 --> 00:02:31.885
- Yeah.

00:02:31.885 --> 00:02:34.921
- But you realize that
this world is becoming

00:02:34.921 --> 00:02:36.222
more and more tech focused.

00:02:36.222 --> 00:02:37.457
And cities have two options.

00:02:37.457 --> 00:02:39.159 align:start size:84% position:16%
They can either
embrace that reality.

00:02:39.159 --> 00:02:44.164 align:start size:97% position:3%
Or they can ignore it to their
peril of their residence.

00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:46.566
- Were you worried at
all that the people

00:02:46.566 --> 00:02:47.834 align:start size:81% position:19%
were gonna come
from San Francisco

00:02:47.834 --> 00:02:50.503
and bring all of the
bad policies and ideas

00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:53.072 align:start size:84% position:16%
that basically wrecked
Silicon Valley,

00:02:53.072 --> 00:02:54.073
and wrecked San Francisco?

00:02:54.073 --> 00:02:55.341
- I hear worry a lot.

00:02:55.341 --> 00:02:56.376
I actually don't worry.

00:02:56.376 --> 00:02:57.443
And I'll tell you why.

00:02:57.443 --> 00:03:00.680 align:start size:88% position:13%
My family unfortunately
was exiled

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:02.182
from a communist
country in Cuba.

00:03:02.182 --> 00:03:04.918 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I don't see people
getting on rafts

00:03:04.918 --> 00:03:06.653
and trying to go back to Cuba.

00:03:06.653 --> 00:03:07.854
You know what I mean?

00:03:07.854 --> 00:03:09.355
It's just not, the
people that come here

00:03:09.355 --> 00:03:11.024
to Miami are traumatized.

00:03:11.024 --> 00:03:13.993 align:start size:97% position:3%
They are traumatized by
governments that don't work.

00:03:13.993 --> 00:03:15.528
By policies that don't work.

00:03:15.528 --> 00:03:18.998
And frankly, the decision
to leave a particular city

00:03:18.998 --> 00:03:21.401
is a very difficult decision.

00:03:21.401 --> 00:03:22.635
Particularly when
you have a family.

00:03:22.635 --> 00:03:23.937 align:start size:81% position:19%
And to uproot your
family and say,

00:03:23.937 --> 00:03:25.138
"I'm going somewhere else."

00:03:25.138 --> 00:03:26.573
That is not easy.

00:03:26.573 --> 00:03:27.807
And so for me.

00:03:27.807 --> 00:03:29.676
I find it very hard to
believe that anybody

00:03:29.676 --> 00:03:30.777 align:start size:88% position:13%
is gonna make that very
difficult decision.

00:03:30.777 --> 00:03:33.613
Basically trauma-based, right?

00:03:33.613 --> 00:03:34.914
To come to a new city.

00:03:34.914 --> 00:03:36.316
And then create
the same policies

00:03:36.316 --> 00:03:37.550
that created the
conditions for them

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.152
to wanna leave in
the first place.

00:03:39.152 --> 00:03:41.554
- What kind of chaos has
it caused in the last year

00:03:41.554 --> 00:03:42.789
being here?

00:03:42.789 --> 00:03:43.856
I mean, real estate
prices are "bananas."

00:03:43.856 --> 00:03:45.091
Rents are going crazy.

00:03:45.091 --> 00:03:46.559
These are all good
set of problems.

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:47.760
But it must be a lot for
you to just have to manage.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:49.195
- You know it's interesting.

00:03:49.195 --> 00:03:51.965 align:start size:84% position:16%
The real estate
prices are "bananas."

00:03:51.965 --> 00:03:54.267
And homes are on the
water, like this home.

00:03:54.267 --> 00:03:55.702
You know what I mean?

00:03:55.702 --> 00:03:58.538 align:start position:0%
But we're comparatively
speaking, incredibly affordable.

00:03:58.538 --> 00:03:59.806
So for example.

00:03:59.806 --> 00:04:01.274 align:start size:81% position:19%
In downtown Miami,
the urban core.

00:04:01.274 --> 00:04:05.078 align:start size:97% position:3%
You can get a one bedroom
apartment in an un-subsidized,

00:04:05.078 --> 00:04:06.613 align:start size:97% position:3%
like not affordable housing
or anything like that.

00:04:06.613 --> 00:04:10.216
Building, brand new, $1,450.

00:04:10.216 --> 00:04:11.784
In Manhattan it's $3,700.

00:04:11.784 --> 00:04:13.353
In San Francisco, it's $3,700.

00:04:13.353 --> 00:04:15.121
So you're talking about a
three to one differential.

00:04:15.121 --> 00:04:17.824 align:start size:94% position:6%
That hasn't changed,
from one day to the next.

00:04:17.824 --> 00:04:21.027
And then we're building a
ton of affordable housing.

00:04:21.027 --> 00:04:23.329 align:start size:97% position:3%
We have the ability to
build supply, which cities.

00:04:23.329 --> 00:04:26.132
Like San Francisco, the
Bay Area oftentimes.

00:04:26.132 --> 00:04:27.567
And New York can't do it.

00:04:27.567 --> 00:04:28.968
Because New York is
sort of overbuilt.

00:04:28.968 --> 00:04:31.838 align:start position:0%
And San Francisco has restricted
their ability to build.

00:04:31.838 --> 00:04:34.073
So we have the ability
to remain competitive

00:04:34.073 --> 00:04:36.142
for decades into the future.

00:04:36.142 --> 00:04:38.578 align:start size:88% position:13%
Which I think is
a huge competitive
advantage for our city.

00:04:38.578 --> 00:04:41.481 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So I think when most people
think of conservatives.

00:04:41.481 --> 00:04:44.017
They think of some
kind of old white guy

00:04:44.017 --> 00:04:45.752
sitting in a smoking room.

00:04:45.752 --> 00:04:48.821
And flipping through the
"Wall Street Journal."

00:04:48.821 --> 00:04:49.922
Or something like that.

00:04:49.922 --> 00:04:51.190
You're a conservative.

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:52.225
It seems to me that
you're sort of what

00:04:52.225 --> 00:04:53.726
the future of conservatism is.

00:04:53.726 --> 00:04:55.128 align:start size:84% position:16%
What does that
actually mean to you?

00:04:55.128 --> 00:04:56.729
What are your kind of
guiding principles?

00:04:56.729 --> 00:04:59.565
- I grew up, I was born in 1977.

00:04:59.565 --> 00:05:01.200
So I grew up sort
of in the eighties.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:02.368
- 76.

00:05:02.368 --> 00:05:03.202
You're a young buck.

00:05:03.202 --> 00:05:04.437
- There you go.

00:05:04.437 --> 00:05:06.539
My dad was mayor
in 1985, of Miami.

00:05:06.539 --> 00:05:08.241
And I grew up sort of
in the Reagan years.

00:05:08.241 --> 00:05:10.643 align:start size:94% position:6%
So for me it's sort of
being a Reagan Republican.

00:05:10.643 --> 00:05:13.946 align:start size:97% position:3%
Was someone that was
strong on national defense.

00:05:13.946 --> 00:05:16.282 align:start size:94% position:6%
For me, I believe in
having a balanced budget.

00:05:16.282 --> 00:05:17.750 align:start size:81% position:19%
Even though that's
something that

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:19.152
our federal government has
not been able to do for long.

00:05:19.152 --> 00:05:20.153
- Are you guys balanced here?

00:05:20.153 --> 00:05:21.087
- We are.

00:05:21.087 --> 00:05:22.488
We actually have a surplus.

00:05:22.488 --> 00:05:23.990 align:start size:97% position:3%
We have a $150 million
surplus in the city of Miami.

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:26.092
We have the second highest
bond rating we've ever had.

00:05:26.092 --> 00:05:27.493
And the highest we've
ever had in the history

00:05:27.493 --> 00:05:28.861
under my leadership.

00:05:28.861 --> 00:05:30.229
So it's a double A plus.

00:05:30.229 --> 00:05:33.499 align:start position:0%
So I believe in fiscal
responsibility for governments.

00:05:33.499 --> 00:05:35.768
I think governments should
live within their means.

00:05:35.768 --> 00:05:38.938 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think that government
should be there

00:05:38.938 --> 00:05:40.673
to facilitate not obstruct.

00:05:40.673 --> 00:05:42.809 align:start size:84% position:16%
And oftentimes they're
obstructionists.

00:05:42.809 --> 00:05:46.512 align:start size:97% position:3%
But as a conservative, I
also, and as a young person.

00:05:46.512 --> 00:05:48.281 align:start size:94% position:6%
And someone who sees,
even young conservatives,

00:05:48.281 --> 00:05:50.983
and young liberals.

00:05:50.983 --> 00:05:52.618 align:start size:84% position:16%
Come up, concerned
about the environment.

00:05:52.618 --> 00:05:53.953
So I think that's something that

00:05:53.953 --> 00:05:56.189 align:start size:88% position:13%
we have to have a
better conversation on.

00:05:56.189 --> 00:05:57.423
Immigration is another issue.

00:05:57.423 --> 00:05:58.658
That we're a city of immigrants.

00:05:58.658 --> 00:06:00.026
We're a country of immigrants.

00:06:00.026 --> 00:06:02.395 align:start size:81% position:19%
That's another
issue that I think

00:06:02.395 --> 00:06:03.863 align:start size:94% position:6%
we need to have a
healthier conversation on

00:06:03.863 --> 00:06:05.331 align:start size:88% position:13%
than the one that
we're having right now.

00:06:05.331 --> 00:06:07.600 align:start size:84% position:16%
In the sort of
national landscape.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:09.035
- Yeah.

00:06:09.035 --> 00:06:11.104 align:start size:97% position:3%
Where does the Bitcoin
stuff fit in this new future

00:06:11.104 --> 00:06:15.007
conservative Mecca that
we've got going here?

00:06:15.007 --> 00:06:16.042
You just did the big conference.

00:06:16.042 --> 00:06:17.243
- Yeah.

00:06:17.243 --> 00:06:18.177 align:start size:84% position:16%
We just did the
biggest, the largest

00:06:18.177 --> 00:06:19.312
Bitcoin conference in the world.

00:06:19.312 --> 00:06:20.513
We also had FTX.

00:06:20.513 --> 00:06:22.648 align:start size:88% position:13%
Which is a large, a
crypto trading platform.

00:06:22.648 --> 00:06:23.916
Out of Hong Kong.

00:06:23.916 --> 00:06:26.486
Name the arena where
the Miami Heat play.

00:06:26.486 --> 00:06:28.421
$200 million naming deal.

00:06:28.421 --> 00:06:30.957 align:start size:84% position:16%
We had Blockchain.com
headquartered in
the city of Miami.

00:06:30.957 --> 00:06:31.824
300 jobs.

00:06:31.824 --> 00:06:33.593
And eToro which is-

00:06:33.593 --> 00:06:35.061 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Don't forget
Locals.com moved here.

00:06:35.061 --> 00:06:36.295
- And Locals.com as well.

00:06:36.295 --> 00:06:37.530
And eToro as well.

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:41.634
So I mean, we've had a
ton of crypto companies

00:06:41.634 --> 00:06:43.136
moving over here.

00:06:43.136 --> 00:06:44.604
And Blockchain and Bitcoin
companies, moving over here.

00:06:44.604 --> 00:06:47.306
Because we believe, we
were sort of the "David,"

00:06:47.306 --> 00:06:49.175 align:start size:84% position:16%
in this "David and
Goliath" story, right?

00:06:49.175 --> 00:06:52.378
But we believe that to be,

00:06:52.378 --> 00:06:54.447 align:start size:88% position:13%
sort of to have that
transformational moment.

00:06:54.447 --> 00:06:56.716
We had to step outside
of the conventional box.

00:06:56.716 --> 00:06:58.184
And I thought that
Bitcoin and crypto

00:06:58.184 --> 00:06:59.585
was our way of doing that.

00:06:59.585 --> 00:07:01.854 align:start size:81% position:19%
We're the first
city in the world,

00:07:01.854 --> 00:07:03.356
sort of the first city
in the United States,

00:07:03.356 --> 00:07:05.258
second city in the world to
put the Satoshi's white paper

00:07:05.258 --> 00:07:06.492
on our website.

00:07:06.492 --> 00:07:07.960
We're the first
city in the world

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:09.462
to begin the process of
allowing our employees

00:07:09.462 --> 00:07:10.963
to get paid in Bitcoin.

00:07:10.963 --> 00:07:14.400
- Do you get all of it?

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:16.736 align:start size:97% position:3%
Or is it just like you have
good people around you?

00:07:16.736 --> 00:07:18.137
Cause some of the tech stuff,

00:07:18.137 --> 00:07:20.573 align:start size:88% position:13%
the high-end, high level
tech stuff is a lot.

00:07:20.573 --> 00:07:21.808
- No, I get it, I get it.

00:07:21.808 --> 00:07:24.177
I get it because, so I
have finance background.

00:07:24.177 --> 00:07:26.712 align:start size:88% position:13%
My dad was an engineer,
is an engineer.

00:07:26.712 --> 00:07:28.748
My grandfather was
an engineer as well.

00:07:28.748 --> 00:07:30.516 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I get the
mathematics behind it.

00:07:30.516 --> 00:07:32.084
I get the principle behind it.

00:07:32.084 --> 00:07:33.986
I was actually part of the
Florida Blockchain Foundation

00:07:33.986 --> 00:07:35.488
when it began.

00:07:35.488 --> 00:07:37.690
And I'm actually part of the
Florida Blockchain Task Force.

00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:39.058
Named by the CFO of Florida.

00:07:39.058 --> 00:07:42.128
To promulgate, state
regulations and laws.

00:07:42.128 --> 00:07:45.698
To hopefully to continue
to bring crypto to Miami.

00:07:45.698 --> 00:07:47.166
And to Florida.

00:07:47.166 --> 00:07:49.202 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Do you have other mayors
in, either in Florida

00:07:49.202 --> 00:07:51.270 align:start size:97% position:3%
or in the rest of the country
that are kind of pissed?

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:52.772
That they're kind of jealous.

00:07:52.772 --> 00:07:54.340
Like this guy is making
it happen, and we're not.

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:56.342
Either cause we're handicapped
by our own state governments

00:07:56.342 --> 00:07:57.844
or whatever it might be.

00:07:57.844 --> 00:08:00.513 align:start size:97% position:3%
- The ones that probably would
be pissed, are not calling.

00:08:01.714 --> 00:08:04.450
I think they feel, I
can't speak for them.

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:05.885
They may feel like they're,

00:08:05.885 --> 00:08:06.819 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they may believe
some of the policies that

00:08:06.819 --> 00:08:08.421
their cities are promulgating.

00:08:08.421 --> 00:08:10.456
That I think are not
good for their cities.

00:08:10.456 --> 00:08:11.724
But I can tell you.

00:08:11.724 --> 00:08:13.526
I've gotten a ton of
mayors that call me

00:08:13.526 --> 00:08:14.794
from across the country.

00:08:14.794 --> 00:08:17.430
And chambers of commerce
from across the country

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:18.898
that call me and say,

00:08:18.898 --> 00:08:20.333 align:start size:97% position:3%
"Hey, how do we replicate
what you're doing down there?"

00:08:20.333 --> 00:08:21.567
And for us.

00:08:21.567 --> 00:08:23.970
They're some things that
are hard to replicate.

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:25.471
Miami is kind of
hard to replicate.

00:08:25.471 --> 00:08:27.073
But there are other things
that I think are very basic.

00:08:27.073 --> 00:08:30.076
For us, basically our formula
for success is three things.

00:08:30.076 --> 00:08:32.078 align:start size:84% position:16%
Keep taxes as low
as humanly possible.

00:08:32.078 --> 00:08:34.280 align:start size:81% position:19%
While other cities
defund police.

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:35.715
We're investing in our police.

00:08:35.715 --> 00:08:37.650 align:start size:97% position:3%
And we have the highest number
of police officers ever.

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:39.385 align:start size:97% position:3%
So public safety is
incredibly important to us.

00:08:39.385 --> 00:08:40.620
It's something that we sell.

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:42.154
Being one of the safest
big cities in America.

00:08:42.154 --> 00:08:44.056
And the third thing is we
focus on quality of life.

00:08:44.056 --> 00:08:45.525 align:start size:88% position:13%
We know the fundamental
truth that nobody

00:08:45.525 --> 00:08:47.527
can live yesterday again.

00:08:47.527 --> 00:08:49.362 align:start size:84% position:16%
So the most important
decision that

00:08:49.362 --> 00:08:50.630
we all make in our lives.

00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:51.564
Is where we are at
this very moment.

00:08:51.564 --> 00:08:52.832
Where we're gonna be tomorrow.

00:08:52.832 --> 00:08:54.066
Where we're gonna
be the day after.

00:08:54.066 --> 00:08:55.234
And if you create
the kind of city

00:08:55.234 --> 00:08:57.470 align:start size:84% position:16%
that really respects
that decision.

00:08:57.470 --> 00:08:58.704
For example.

00:08:58.704 --> 00:09:00.039
We have 555 homeless
in the city of Miami.

00:09:00.039 --> 00:09:01.574
We wanna get to functional zero.

00:09:01.574 --> 00:09:03.509 align:start size:97% position:3%
Which means we wanna be
one of the first big cities

00:09:03.509 --> 00:09:05.411
in America, not to
have any homeless.

00:09:05.411 --> 00:09:06.612
- I mean, I've been
here for five days.

00:09:06.612 --> 00:09:08.014
I have not seen one
homeless person.

00:09:08.014 --> 00:09:09.015
I come from LA.

00:09:09.015 --> 00:09:09.949
It's very, very different.

00:09:09.949 --> 00:09:11.150
- It's very different.

00:09:11.150 --> 00:09:12.685 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I think, we've done
a variety of things

00:09:12.685 --> 00:09:14.954
over a number of years
to get to that point.

00:09:14.954 --> 00:09:16.722
And we wanna get to zero.

00:09:16.722 --> 00:09:17.957
- For example.

00:09:17.957 --> 00:09:19.225
What's one thing you've
done on the homeless?

00:09:19.225 --> 00:09:21.260
That I can bring back
with me to California.

00:09:21.260 --> 00:09:22.495 align:start size:94% position:6%
- We have something
called the Homeless Trust.

00:09:22.495 --> 00:09:24.697 align:start size:81% position:19%
We spend about $50
million a year.

00:09:24.697 --> 00:09:26.499
It's a food and beverage tax.

00:09:26.499 --> 00:09:29.135
Basically a tourist tax it's
not paid for by our residents.

00:09:29.135 --> 00:09:31.037
But it generates money.

00:09:31.037 --> 00:09:32.772
And we use that money
to build facilities.

00:09:32.772 --> 00:09:35.675
To treat the homeless
in a comprehensive way.

00:09:35.675 --> 00:09:36.876
They're obviously,
a lot of them have

00:09:36.876 --> 00:09:38.244 align:start size:84% position:16%
mental health and
drug addiction issues.

00:09:38.244 --> 00:09:39.545
And vocational training.

00:09:39.545 --> 00:09:42.214
So that they can leave a shelter

00:09:42.214 --> 00:09:44.317
with the ability
to make a living

00:09:44.317 --> 00:09:45.618
and reintegrate into society.

00:09:45.618 --> 00:09:47.353
So they get mental
health counseling.

00:09:47.353 --> 00:09:48.588 align:start size:84% position:16%
They get drug
addiction counseling.

00:09:48.588 --> 00:09:50.856 align:start size:84% position:16%
And they also get
vocational training.

00:09:50.856 --> 00:09:53.993
- When you see the mayors
of say, LA, San Francisco,

00:09:53.993 --> 00:09:55.795
Seattle, Portland.

00:09:55.795 --> 00:09:57.830
All the progressive mayors
that are just raising taxes.

00:09:57.830 --> 00:10:00.099 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then you see the reverse
of all of the policies

00:10:00.099 --> 00:10:01.634
that you've got working here.

00:10:01.634 --> 00:10:03.569
Do you ever think maybe I
could just call these guys

00:10:03.569 --> 00:10:06.205 align:start size:97% position:3%
and explain basic economics
to them or something?

00:10:06.205 --> 00:10:07.907 align:start size:94% position:6%
I just never can
understand the disconnect

00:10:07.907 --> 00:10:10.309 align:start size:81% position:19%
between the things
that they say.

00:10:10.309 --> 00:10:11.944
And then the results
that end up happening.

00:10:11.944 --> 00:10:13.613
- A lot of them are my friends.

00:10:13.613 --> 00:10:16.949
And I'm gonna be, if
reelected in November.

00:10:16.949 --> 00:10:19.185
Which hopefully will be
a non-event God willing.

00:10:19.185 --> 00:10:21.420
- You got elected with 86%.

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:23.522
And I mean, what
are we gonna we do-

00:10:25.124 --> 00:10:26.692
- We gotta up man.

00:10:26.692 --> 00:10:28.227
I'll tell you a funny
story about that later.

00:10:28.227 --> 00:10:31.530
But I think, if re-elected
I'm gonna be the president

00:10:31.530 --> 00:10:32.632
Of the US Conference of Mayors.

00:10:32.632 --> 00:10:33.899
So one of the cool things is.

00:10:33.899 --> 00:10:36.469
My colleagues, those mayors.

00:10:36.469 --> 00:10:39.905
Have chosen me to be the
president of all the mayors

00:10:39.905 --> 00:10:41.407
in the United States.

00:10:41.407 --> 00:10:42.908 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think it's a really
wonderful opportunity

00:10:42.908 --> 00:10:44.877 align:start size:97% position:3%
to take this formula that
has been successful in Miami.

00:10:44.877 --> 00:10:46.379
And export it to urban America.

00:10:46.379 --> 00:10:49.281 align:start position:0%
I think there's some things
that we can do on gun violence.

00:10:49.281 --> 00:10:50.850 align:start position:0%
I think there's some things
that you can do on homelessness.

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:55.121
And frankly, I'm willing to
talk to anybody, any mayor.

00:10:55.121 --> 00:10:56.088
And give them advice.

00:10:56.088 --> 00:10:57.690
But sometimes I think it's hard.

00:10:57.690 --> 00:11:00.226
Because it's either
what they believe.

00:11:00.226 --> 00:11:01.594 align:start size:97% position:3%
Which is sort of
antithetical to what I think

00:11:01.594 --> 00:11:02.828
is a growth policy.

00:11:02.828 --> 00:11:04.830
Or what the residents believe.

00:11:04.830 --> 00:11:06.065
Which oftentimes make it hard.

00:11:06.065 --> 00:11:07.333
Because they're
sort of boxed in.

00:11:07.333 --> 00:11:08.567
- Right.

00:11:08.567 --> 00:11:10.069
Where does DeSantis
fit into all this?

00:11:10.069 --> 00:11:12.605
It seems like from again,
for me 3000 miles away.

00:11:12.605 --> 00:11:15.408
That's the best governor
in the country right now.

00:11:15.408 --> 00:11:16.642
- Yeah.

00:11:16.642 --> 00:11:18.144
I think he did some
things that were very

00:11:18.144 --> 00:11:20.279
sort of counter
narrative, right.

00:11:20.279 --> 00:11:22.314
And open the state
up a lot earlier than

00:11:22.314 --> 00:11:24.684
a lot of people thought what
would have been a good idea.

00:11:24.684 --> 00:11:26.285
And it turned out to
be great for the city.

00:11:26.285 --> 00:11:27.553 align:start size:84% position:16%
And certainly great
for the state.

00:11:27.553 --> 00:11:29.588 align:start size:84% position:16%
And he's gotten a
ton of kudos on that.

00:11:29.588 --> 00:11:33.893 align:start position:0%
And I think, I pretty sure
that if he's reelected in 2022,

00:11:33.893 --> 00:11:36.529
which I think is also, he's
got a very good shot of that.

00:11:36.529 --> 00:11:39.465
2024, I wouldn't be
surprised if he puts

00:11:39.465 --> 00:11:40.833
his name in he ring.

00:11:40.833 --> 00:11:42.401
- I saw a little
glimmer in the eye.

00:11:42.401 --> 00:11:43.703 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think you might
know something there.

00:11:43.703 --> 00:11:45.805
Where does that put you then?

00:11:45.805 --> 00:11:49.608
- Look, I've, again I
have to get reelected.

00:11:49.608 --> 00:11:51.110 align:start size:84% position:16%
And obviously I have
a year and a half

00:11:51.110 --> 00:11:52.878
as hopefully president
US Conference of Mayors.

00:11:52.878 --> 00:11:55.214 align:start size:84% position:16%
But I've talked about
this in the past.

00:11:55.214 --> 00:11:57.383
There's only a few things
that I'd be interested

00:11:57.383 --> 00:11:58.384
in doing beyond being mayor.

00:11:58.384 --> 00:11:59.919
Which has been an amazing thing.

00:11:59.919 --> 00:12:01.687 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
Being governor of State
of Florida in 2026,

00:12:01.687 --> 00:12:04.623 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
it's something that I
would certainly consider.

00:12:04.623 --> 00:12:05.925 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Does it ever seem
odd to you that

00:12:05.925 --> 00:12:07.126
you're governor of all things.

00:12:07.126 --> 00:12:08.394
Like somebody who gets it.

00:12:08.394 --> 00:12:09.929
You get business and
all of those things.

00:12:09.929 --> 00:12:11.764
I know there's a
lot of good people.

00:12:11.764 --> 00:12:13.733
Usually kind of right-leaning.

00:12:13.733 --> 00:12:15.401
Who just don't wanna
go into government.

00:12:15.401 --> 00:12:17.336 align:start size:97% position:3%
Because they don't believe
that government is everything.

00:12:17.336 --> 00:12:19.338
So then they just don't do it.

00:12:19.338 --> 00:12:20.773
And then we get worse
and worse politicians.

00:12:20.773 --> 00:12:21.707 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, that's part
of the problem.

00:12:21.707 --> 00:12:22.775
Government isn't everything.

00:12:22.775 --> 00:12:24.210
And shouldn't be everything.

00:12:24.210 --> 00:12:25.644 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think part of the
issue we have in our society.

00:12:25.644 --> 00:12:27.079 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is people think that
government needs to solve

00:12:27.079 --> 00:12:28.047
all our problems.

00:12:28.047 --> 00:12:29.181
It's not the way it works.

00:12:29.181 --> 00:12:30.382 align:start size:84% position:16%
Government was
supposed to be created

00:12:30.382 --> 00:12:31.917
for a limited amount of reasons.

00:12:31.917 --> 00:12:33.786
The things that we're good
at are core competencies.

00:12:33.786 --> 00:12:35.454
And then, when you
have good leaders

00:12:35.454 --> 00:12:36.689
that lead in government.

00:12:36.689 --> 00:12:38.157
They can work with
the private sector.

00:12:38.157 --> 00:12:40.993 align:start size:97% position:3%
With the philanthropic sectors
to deal with big problems.

00:12:40.993 --> 00:12:42.561
Like homelessness and others.

00:12:42.561 --> 00:12:45.264
But I think, there's
this misperception

00:12:45.264 --> 00:12:46.932 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you just keep growing
the size of government.

00:12:46.932 --> 00:12:49.668 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that it can somehow
solve a bunch of problems.

00:12:49.668 --> 00:12:51.604
That government has been
historically unable to do.

00:12:51.604 --> 00:12:55.107 align:start size:97% position:3%
- It put band-aids on
problems it's created, right?

00:12:55.107 --> 00:12:55.941
- Exactly.

00:12:55.941 --> 00:12:56.776
- That's pretty much it.

00:12:56.776 --> 00:12:57.743
- Exactly.

00:12:57.743 --> 00:12:58.577
Or make the problems worse.

00:12:58.577 --> 00:12:59.612
- Or make them worse.

00:12:59.612 --> 00:13:01.647
What's been sort
of unexpected as

00:13:01.647 --> 00:13:02.815
this all has blown up here.

00:13:02.815 --> 00:13:05.084
I guess either on good or bad.

00:13:05.084 --> 00:13:07.386
Just generally so much at once.

00:13:07.386 --> 00:13:08.888
- Yeah.

00:13:08.888 --> 00:13:10.689
For me, if you would've told
me on December 3rd, right?

00:13:10.689 --> 00:13:12.258
That the next day I was
gonna put out a tweet

00:13:12.258 --> 00:13:14.560
that was gonna be, they call it

00:13:14.560 --> 00:13:15.861
"the tweet heard
around the world."

00:13:15.861 --> 00:13:16.796
- Yeah.

00:13:16.796 --> 00:13:17.997
- And how can I help tweet.

00:13:17.997 --> 00:13:19.465
In terms of making
Miami Silicon Valley.

00:13:19.465 --> 00:13:22.067
And that that was gonna get
2.7 million impressions.

00:13:22.067 --> 00:13:24.870
- Honestly, I don't think
I knew of you until then.

00:13:24.870 --> 00:13:27.139 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that day suddenly everyone
I knew in the tech world

00:13:27.139 --> 00:13:28.607
was like, that's the guy,
that's the city, let's go.

00:13:28.607 --> 00:13:30.609
And then it's just been, yeah.

00:13:30.609 --> 00:13:31.610
- It's been crazy.

00:13:31.610 --> 00:13:32.845
Ever since then.

00:13:32.845 --> 00:13:34.547
I mean, I found what
I call in Twitter,

00:13:34.547 --> 00:13:36.048
a portal of positivity.

00:13:36.048 --> 00:13:37.550
You know what I mean?

00:13:37.550 --> 00:13:39.618 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I tweeted 800 more times
in the month of December.

00:13:40.553 --> 00:13:41.787
Which got 27 million.

00:13:41.787 --> 00:13:43.255
- Generally, that's
not a good thing.

00:13:43.255 --> 00:13:48.127 align:start size:94% position:6%
- And this is gonna sound
really "hokey pokey."

00:13:48.861 --> 00:13:50.095
What I'm gonna tell you.

00:13:50.095 --> 00:13:50.963
But in many ways
I found my voice.

00:13:50.963 --> 00:13:52.031
You know what I mean?

00:13:52.031 --> 00:13:54.233
It kind of sounds, book clubish.

00:13:54.233 --> 00:13:56.068
But I really did.

00:13:56.068 --> 00:13:58.337 align:start size:84% position:16%
I was able to really
express myself.

00:13:58.337 --> 00:14:02.474 align:start size:97% position:3%
Be myself without the
usual concerns and worries,

00:14:02.474 --> 00:14:03.542
"Hey, I'm gonna make a mistake?"

00:14:03.542 --> 00:14:05.110
Am I gonna say something wrong?

00:14:05.110 --> 00:14:07.880 align:start size:97% position:3%
Usually if you look at elected
officials in social media.

00:14:07.880 --> 00:14:09.114
It's very antiseptic.

00:14:09.114 --> 00:14:10.716 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's like they went
and then cut a ribbon.

00:14:10.716 --> 00:14:11.784
Or they went and they did this.

00:14:11.784 --> 00:14:13.419
They don't have a personality.

00:14:13.419 --> 00:14:14.954 align:start size:84% position:16%
They can't really
express themselves,
cause they're afraid.

00:14:14.954 --> 00:14:15.921
They're afraid of
making a mistake.

00:14:15.921 --> 00:14:17.122
Or saying the wrong thing.

00:14:17.122 --> 00:14:18.591 align:start size:88% position:13%
And at some point I just
said, you know what.

00:14:18.591 --> 00:14:19.592
I'm gonna go all in.

00:14:20.492 --> 00:14:21.493
Let's see how this goes.

00:14:21.493 --> 00:14:22.328
- Yeah.

00:14:22.328 --> 00:14:23.162
- And it's been great.

00:14:23.162 --> 00:14:24.396
It's been a lot of fun.

00:14:24.396 --> 00:14:27.499 align:start size:81% position:19%
I've, it's part
of what allowed me

00:14:27.499 --> 00:14:28.968 align:start size:84% position:16%
to get into the
crypto space as well.

00:14:28.968 --> 00:14:30.336
At the level that I did.

00:14:30.336 --> 00:14:33.038
Because I was really
getting great feedback.

00:14:33.038 --> 00:14:34.340
And I said, I gotta do more.

00:14:34.340 --> 00:14:35.307
- Were you kinda shocked?

00:14:35.307 --> 00:14:36.542
So you put that tweet out.

00:14:36.542 --> 00:14:38.010 align:start size:81% position:19%
And then it was
basically everyone

00:14:38.010 --> 00:14:40.379 align:start size:97% position:3%
that I know in the tech world,
was just like, that's it.

00:14:40.379 --> 00:14:42.348 align:start size:94% position:6%
You just shocked that they
immediately were like,

00:14:42.348 --> 00:14:43.883
what can I do?

00:14:43.883 --> 00:14:44.884
And then you basically just
set up meetings with people.

00:14:44.884 --> 00:14:45.718
Right?

00:14:45.718 --> 00:14:47.253
- I was shocked.

00:14:47.253 --> 00:14:50.756
But it was also a tremendous
learning experience, right?

00:14:50.756 --> 00:14:53.926 align:start size:88% position:13%
In the sense that I
realized what this world

00:14:53.926 --> 00:14:55.127
was all about now.

00:14:55.127 --> 00:14:56.662
This world is highly,
highly disruptive.

00:14:56.662 --> 00:14:57.930
I mean I gotta say this.

00:14:57.930 --> 00:14:59.732
I don't even know what
camera to look into.

00:15:00.599 --> 00:15:01.433
- Somewhere over there.

00:15:01.433 --> 00:15:02.635
- There, here.

00:15:02.635 --> 00:15:03.836
This world is highly,
highly disruptive.

00:15:03.836 --> 00:15:06.972 align:start size:84% position:16%
To the point where
the paradigm can shift

00:15:06.972 --> 00:15:08.474
on you in a second.

00:15:08.474 --> 00:15:09.909
- Yeah.

00:15:09.909 --> 00:15:11.110 align:start size:94% position:6%
- And I think that's
what some of these cities

00:15:11.110 --> 00:15:12.378 align:start size:81% position:19%
are not completely
understanding.

00:15:12.378 --> 00:15:16.582
And I think that as
you see what I call

00:15:16.582 --> 00:15:19.285 align:start size:94% position:6%
this confluence of capital
coming from New York,

00:15:19.285 --> 00:15:22.087 align:start size:97% position:3%
Silicon Valley, and Miami's
geo position, right?

00:15:22.087 --> 00:15:24.123
Close to South America,
and Central America.

00:15:24.123 --> 00:15:28.594 align:start size:88% position:13%
Close to the Middle
East, Israel and Europe.

00:15:28.594 --> 00:15:31.163
We're sort of in the
middle of all that.

00:15:31.163 --> 00:15:33.832 align:start position:0%
We're the place that can really
be that global mega city.

00:15:33.832 --> 00:15:36.869
That can be what I call
the capital of capital.

00:15:36.869 --> 00:15:38.337
The place where if you
wanna build a business

00:15:38.337 --> 00:15:39.271
or start a business.

00:15:39.271 --> 00:15:40.506
You gotta be living in Miami.

00:15:40.506 --> 00:15:42.007
- No Taxes?

00:15:42.007 --> 00:15:44.877 align:start size:97% position:3%
Does it all sort of boil down
to that at the beginning?

00:15:44.877 --> 00:15:46.845
- That's a part of the equation.

00:15:46.845 --> 00:15:48.113
But it's not all
of the equation.

00:15:48.113 --> 00:15:49.949
I mean, I think for
us it was low taxes.

00:15:49.949 --> 00:15:51.450
It's incredible weather.

00:15:51.450 --> 00:15:53.652
It's incredible amenities
from a city perspective.

00:15:53.652 --> 00:15:54.887
Now the restaurants.

00:15:54.887 --> 00:15:56.422
Everything is sort
of coming together.

00:15:56.422 --> 00:16:00.292
And I think, and it's gonna
sound again, sort of crazy.

00:16:00.292 --> 00:16:02.394
But it's being welcoming, right?

00:16:02.394 --> 00:16:03.963
I can't tell you how many
people that have told me

00:16:03.963 --> 00:16:07.666 align:start size:94% position:6%
look, I would stay, living
in one of these cities.

00:16:07.666 --> 00:16:08.734
I'm still paying a lot of taxes.

00:16:08.734 --> 00:16:09.902
I'm not getting the services.

00:16:09.902 --> 00:16:10.970 align:start size:88% position:13%
I'm not getting a
